## King of the gods ⚡

God of the **sky, thunder, and justice**. His symbols include the thunderbolt and eagle.

{#0F766E}Brother of Poseidon and Hades; ruler of Mount Olympus.{/}

## Queen of the gods 👑

Goddess of **marriage and queenship**; wife and sister of Zeus. The peacock and crown are her best-known attributes.

{#0F766E}Many myths show her punishing Zeus's lovers or their children, especially Heracles.{/}

## God of the sea 🌊

Rules the **sea, earthquakes, and horses**. He carries a trident and is brother to Zeus and Hades.

{#0F766E}His anger drives much of Odysseus's difficult voyage home.{/}

## Goddess of the harvest 🌾

Rules **agriculture, grain, and fertility of the earth**.

{#0F766E}Her search for her daughter Persephone explains the seasonal cycle in a famous myth.{/}

## Goddess of wisdom 🦉

Rules **wisdom, strategic warfare, and skilled crafts**; patron of Athens.

{#0F766E}Recognized by her owl, helmet, spear, olive tree, and aegis.{/}

## From Zeus's head

Athena sprang out **fully grown and armed** after Zeus swallowed her pregnant mother, Metis.

{#0F766E}The strange birth emphasizes Athena's connection to intellect and strategy.{/}

## God of prophecy and music ☀️

Also rules **healing, archery, and plague**. He is the twin brother of Artemis.

{#0F766E}His great oracle spoke at Delphi; the lyre and laurel are key symbols.{/}

## Goddess of the hunt 🏹

Protects the **wilderness, wild animals, and childbirth** and is often linked with the moon.

{#0F766E}Twin of Apollo; recognized by her bow, deer, and hunting companions.{/}

## Goddess of love 💕

Rules **beauty, desire, and attraction**. Doves, roses, and the sea are her symbols.

{#0F766E}Traditions differ: she is either born from sea foam or is a daughter of Zeus and Dione.{/}

## God of brutal war ⚔️

Embodies the **violent and chaotic** side of battle, unlike Athena's strategy.

{#0F766E}Son of Zeus and Hera and famously linked romantically with Aphrodite.{/}

## Smith of the gods 🔨

God of **fire, metalworking, and craftsmanship**. He creates divine armor, weapons, and machines.

{#0F766E}He is married to Aphrodite in the best-known tradition.{/}

## Messenger of the gods 🪽

Protects **travelers, trade, thieves, and clever speech**. He also escorts souls to the Underworld.

{#0F766E}Look for winged sandals and the herald's staff called the caduceus.{/}

## God of wine and theater 🍇

Rules **wine, ecstasy, theater, and transformation**.

{#0F766E}Grapevines and ivy surround him; satyrs and maenads join his wild processions.{/}

## Goddess of the hearth 🔥

Protects the **home, household, and sacred fire** at the center of family and civic life.

{#0F766E}Some lists give her Olympian seat to Dionysus; ancient traditions were not fully uniform.{/}

## Ruler of the Underworld 💀

God of the **dead and wealth beneath the earth**; brother of Zeus and Poseidon.

{#0F766E}Hades is stern and feared, but Greek myth does not treat him as a god of evil.{/}

## Queen of the Underworld 🌱

Daughter of Demeter and wife of Hades. She moves between the worlds of the living and dead.

{#0F766E}Her return brings growth; her absence is associated with the barren season.{/}

## The older divine generation

Children of **Gaia and Uranus**, they ruled before the Olympians. Cronus was their best-known leader.

{#0F766E}Zeus's generation overthrew them in the war called the Titanomachy.{/}

## Titan father of the Olympians

Cronus ruled before Zeus. To prevent a prophecy of overthrow, he **swallowed his newborn children**.

{#0F766E}Rhea saved Zeus, who later freed his siblings and defeated Cronus.{/}

## Mother of the first Olympians

Titan wife of Cronus and mother of Zeus and his five elder siblings.

{#0F766E}She hid baby Zeus on Crete and tricked Cronus with a stone wrapped like an infant.{/}

## Earth and Sky 🌍

**Gaia** is the primordial Earth; **Uranus** is the Sky. Their children include the Titans, Cyclopes, and Hundred-Handers.

{#0F766E}They stand near the beginning of Greek myth's succession of divine generations.{/}

## Titan champion of humanity 🔥

Prometheus **steals divine fire** for mortals and is punished by Zeus.

{#0F766E}His name became a symbol of bold invention, foresight, and defiance of authority.{/}

## God of desire 🏹

His arrows cause overwhelming **love or attraction**.

{#0F766E}Later myth often calls him Aphrodite's son; earlier tradition can make Eros a primordial creative force.{/}

## Three rulers of fate 🧵

**Clotho** spins life's thread, **Lachesis** measures it, and **Atropos** cuts it.

{#0F766E}They express the limits of destiny that even divine power cannot easily escape.{/}

## Goddesses of inspiration 🎶

The **nine Muses** preside over poetry, music, history, dance, and other arts and learning.

{#0F766E}Epic poets often begin by asking a Muse to help them sing the tale.{/}

## Winged Victory 🪽

Nike personifies **victory** in war, athletics, and competition.

{#0F766E}She is shown with wings and often carries a victory wreath or palm branch.{/}

## Goddess of retribution ⚖️

Nemesis restores balance when **hubris**, excessive pride or overreaching, goes unpunished.

{#0F766E}She represents consequences, not random revenge.{/}

## God of the wild and shepherds 🐐

A rustic, goat-legged god of **flocks, mountains, and untamed nature**.

{#0F766E}His sudden terrifying cry inspired the word “panic”; he plays the panpipes.{/}

## Greece's great strongman 🦁

Son of Zeus and the mortal Alcmene, famous for the **Twelve Labours**.

{#0F766E}His lion skin and club make him instantly recognizable.{/}

## Slayer of Medusa 🛡️

Perseus uses divine gifts and a mirrored shield to behead Medusa **without looking directly at her**.

{#0F766E}He later rescues and marries Andromeda.{/}

## Slayer of the Minotaur 🧵

Athenian hero who kills the Minotaur inside the Labyrinth.

{#0F766E}Ariadne's thread enables his escape, but Theseus later abandons her on Naxos.{/}

## Greatest Greek warrior at Troy ⚔️

Achilles is the central hero of the **Iliad**, defined by unmatched skill, fierce pride, and a short destined life.

{#0F766E}His withdrawal and return to battle shape the war's most famous episode.{/}

## In a later tradition

Thetis dips him in the Styx but the heel she holds stays vulnerable—source of “**Achilles' heel**.”

{#0F766E}This detail is absent from Homer's Iliad, where Achilles is extraordinary but not described this way.{/}

## Clever king of Ithaca ⛵

Hero of the **Odyssey**, known for strategy, persuasion, disguise, and endurance.

{#0F766E}He devises the Trojan Horse but angers Poseidon during his long journey home.{/}

## Leader of the Argonauts 🚢

Jason sails to Colchis to win the **Golden Fleece** and reclaim his throne.

{#0F766E}Medea helps him survive impossible trials; his later betrayal of her brings disaster.{/}

## Musician who charmed the Underworld 🎵

Orpheus's song moves all creation and persuades Hades to release **Eurydice**.

{#0F766E}One condition defeats him: he must not look back before they reach daylight.{/}

## Inventor and reckless son 🪽

**Daedalus** builds the Labyrinth and wings for escape; **Icarus** ignores his warning and flies too high.

{#0F766E}The story is a lasting image of invention, freedom, and dangerous overconfidence.{/}

## King with the golden touch ✨

Midas asks Dionysus to make everything he touches **turn to gold**.

{#0F766E}The wish teaches that wealth without judgment can destroy what truly matters.{/}

## The face linked to the Trojan War

Wife of Menelaus whose departure with **Paris of Troy** triggers the Greek expedition.

{#0F766E}Ancient versions differ over how willingly she went and even whether the real Helen reached Troy.{/}

## Clever queen of Ithaca 🧶

Penelope resists remarriage during Odysseus's twenty-year absence by **weaving and secretly unweaving** a shroud.

{#0F766E}Her caution and intelligence parallel her husband's famous cunning.{/}

## Guide through the Labyrinth 🧵

Daughter of King Minos who gives Theseus a ball of thread and helps him defeat the Minotaur.

{#0F766E}After Theseus abandons her, many versions unite her with Dionysus.{/}

## Prophet no one believes 🔮

Cassandra always speaks **true warnings**, yet her curse ensures others dismiss them.

{#0F766E}She foresees Troy's destruction, making her a symbol of ignored truth.{/}

## Sorceress who aids Jason 🧪

Medea uses magic and inside knowledge to help Jason seize the **Golden Fleece**.

{#0F766E}Her later story explores exile, betrayal, and terrible revenge.{/}

## King trapped by prophecy

Oedipus solves the Sphinx's riddle but unknowingly fulfills the fate he tried to escape.

{#0F766E}His tragedy asks whether knowledge and choice can overcome destiny.{/}

## Achilles' closest companion

Patroclus enters battle in Achilles' armor and is killed by **Hector**.

{#0F766E}Grief for Patroclus transforms Achilles' anger and sends him back to fight.{/}

## The mortal Gorgon 🐍

Medusa's gaze turns viewers to **stone**. Perseus approaches by watching her reflection in Athena's shield.

{#0F766E}Ancient accounts differ about her origin; later versions make her transformed by Athena.{/}

## Stheno, Euryale, and Medusa

Three fearsome sisters with petrifying power. **Only Medusa is mortal**, which makes her Perseus's target.

{#0F766E}The Gorgon head later becomes the protective image on Athena's aegis.{/}

## Bull-headed monster of Crete 🐂

The Minotaur lives inside **Daedalus's Labyrinth**, where Athens must send victims.

{#0F766E}Theseus defeats it and follows Ariadne's thread back out.{/}

## Regenerating many-headed serpent 🐉

Cut off one head and more grow back. Heracles and Iolaus **seal each neck with fire**.

{#0F766E}Heracles then uses its venom on his arrows—an advantage with tragic consequences.{/}

## Hound of the Underworld 🐕

Cerberus guards Hades' realm so the dead cannot escape. Art usually gives him **three heads**.

{#0F766E}Heracles subdues him without weapons and later returns him unharmed.{/}

## One-eyed giants 👁️

Greek tradition has more than one kind: divine craftsmen forge the gods' weapons, while **Polyphemus** is a brutal shepherd in the Odyssey.

{#0F766E}Not every Cyclops belongs to exactly the same story or family.{/}

## Singers who lure sailors 🎶

Their irresistible voices draw ships toward destruction. In early Greek art they are often **woman-bird hybrids**, not mermaids.

{#0F766E}Odysseus hears them while bound to his mast.{/}

## Winged snatchers of the storm 🌪️

Harpies are **woman-bird spirits** who carry people or objects away.

{#0F766E}The Argonauts encounter them while helping the tormented prophet Phineus.{/}

## Fire-breathing hybrid 🔥

Usually part **lion, goat, and snake**, the Chimera ravages Lycia.

{#0F766E}Bellerophon attacks it from above while riding Pegasus.{/}

## Riddle-keeper of Thebes 🦁

A woman-headed, winged lion who destroys those unable to answer her riddle.

{#0F766E}Oedipus solves it: the creature that walks on four, two, then three legs is a human across life's stages.{/}

## Two dangers, one narrow passage 🌊

**Scylla** snatches sailors; **Charybdis** swallows the sea in a vast whirlpool.

{#0F766E}“Between Scylla and Charybdis” means choosing between two serious dangers.{/}

## The winged horse 🪽

Pegasus springs from Medusa and later carries **Bellerophon** into battle against the Chimera.

{#0F766E}He is also connected with springs and poetic inspiration.{/}

## Half human, half horse 🐎

Centaurs often symbolize untamed appetite and conflict with civilization.

{#0F766E}The wise Chiron is an important exception and tutors many heroes.{/}

## Troubles escape from a jar

Pandora opens a forbidden **jar**, releasing suffering into the world; **hope remains** within.

{#0F766E}“Pandora's box” is famous, but the Greek object was a large storage jar, or pithos.{/}

## To empower humanity 🔥

Prometheus steals divine fire so mortals can develop **crafts, tools, and civilization**.

{#0F766E}Zeus punishes both the Titan and humanity, linking this myth directly with Pandora.{/}

## Loss, return, and renewal 🌱

While Persephone is below, Demeter's grief makes the land barren; her return restores growth.

{#0F766E}Pomegranate seeds bind Persephone to spend part of each year with Hades; details vary by source.{/}

## Icarus ignores the middle path

Their handmade wings work, but Icarus flies too close to the sun; the wax fails and he falls.

{#0F766E}Daedalus survives, turning triumph in invention into personal tragedy.{/}

## He looks back too soon

Orpheus must trust that Eurydice follows behind him, but turns before the condition is fulfilled.

{#0F766E}The myth joins the power of art with the human difficulty of doubt and patience.{/}

## He falls for his own reflection

Narcissus cannot leave the beautiful image in the pool and **wastes away** beside it.

{#0F766E}A narcissus flower marks the spot; the myth gives modern culture the term narcissism.{/}

## Hera limits her voice

The nymph Echo can repeat only the **last words spoken by others**.

{#0F766E}Her unreturned love for Narcissus links two stories about failed communication and self-absorption.{/}

## A weaving contest with Athena 🕷️

Arachne's extraordinary skill is matched by **hubris** toward the goddess of crafts.

{#0F766E}The transformation makes her and her descendants eternal weavers; details differ among retellings.{/}

## An endless uphill task 🪨

Sisyphus eternally pushes a boulder toward a summit, only to watch it **roll down again**.

{#0F766E}A “Sisyphean” task is exhausting, repetitive, and never completed.{/}

## Forever near, never satisfied

Water and fruit remain just beyond Tantalus's reach in the Underworld.

{#0F766E}To “tantalize” is to tempt someone with something visible but unattainable.{/}

## He washes it away

Midas repents, and Dionysus directs him to bathe in the **Pactolus River**.

{#0F766E}The river's legendary gold-bearing sands were explained by the power leaving Midas.{/}

## He judges against Apollo 🎶

Midas favors Pan's rustic pipes over Apollo's lyre, so Apollo gives him **donkey ears**.

{#0F766E}This is a separate Midas story from the golden touch.{/}

## Atonement through impossible tasks

After a family tragedy caused by Hera's madness, Heracles serves **King Eurystheus** to seek purification.

{#0F766E}Two completed tasks are disallowed, turning the original ten into twelve.{/}

## Strength, not weapons 🦁

Heracles wrestles the invulnerable lion, then wears its hide as armor.

{#0F766E}The lion skin becomes his most recognizable attribute.{/}

## He redirects two rivers 🌊

Instead of shoveling, Heracles uses **engineering** to flush years of waste away in one day.

{#0F766E}The feat shows intelligence as well as strength.{/}

## A voyage to reclaim a throne 🚢

Jason and the Argonauts sail aboard the **Argo** to Colchis. Medea enables him to pass the king's trials and escape with the Fleece.

{#0F766E}The expedition gathers many famous heroes into one generation.{/}

## Helen's departure with Paris

Paris chooses Aphrodite in a divine beauty contest; she promises him Helen. Greek rulers bound by an oath then join **Menelaus** against Troy.

{#0F766E}Ancient accounts differ over Helen's choice, but divine rivalry consistently fuels the war.{/}

## The rage of Achilles

The **Iliad** covers weeks in the war's tenth year, from Achilles' quarrel to his meeting with King Priam.

{#0F766E}It ends before the Trojan Horse and the fall of Troy.{/}

## A deceptive gift 🐴

Greek soldiers hide inside a wooden horse while the fleet pretends to leave. Once inside Troy, they **open the gates at night**.

{#0F766E}Cassandra and Laocoön warn against the horse, but are ignored.{/}

## A ten-year struggle to get home ⛵

Odysseus survives monsters and divine opposition while **Penelope and Telemachus** resist the suitors in Ithaca.

{#0F766E}The epic is about homecoming, identity, endurance, and hospitality as much as adventure.{/}

## “Nobody” and the sheep 👁️

Odysseus blinds the Cyclops and ties his men beneath sheep to pass the doorway unseen.

{#0F766E}His clever plan succeeds, but his prideful boast brings Poseidon's long punishment.{/}

## Home of the Olympian gods ⛰️

The gods feast, debate, and observe mortals from Olympus, ruled by Zeus.

{#0F766E}It is both a real Greek mountain and an imagined divine dwelling.{/}

## The realm of the dead

Hades and Persephone rule a shadowy world reached after death.

{#0F766E}It contains Tartarus and Elysium, but the Underworld as a whole is not a place only for the wicked.{/}

## Sacred river of the Underworld

The Styx marks a boundary and guarantees the gods' most serious **oaths**.

{#0F766E}Accounts name several infernal rivers, and details of the crossing vary over time.{/}

## The deepest abyss

Tartarus is both a primordial depth and a prison for enemies of the gods.

{#0F766E}It is a specific region, not another name for the entire Underworld.{/}

## The blessed afterlife 🌿

**Elysium** or the Elysian Fields offers peace and happiness to a favored dead.

{#0F766E}Who qualifies changes across ancient sources, reflecting evolving ideas of the afterlife.{/}

## Apollo's great oracle 🔮

At Delphi, the **Pythia** answers questions for visitors from across the Greek world.

{#0F766E}Myth marks Delphi as the world's center with the stone called the omphalos, or “navel.”{/}

## Island of Zeus and the Labyrinth 🐂

Crete links the childhood of **Zeus** with the stories of Minos, Daedalus, Ariadne, Theseus, and the Minotaur.

{#0F766E}It is one of mythology's richest crossroads of connected stories.{/}

## The home Odysseus seeks 🏠

Small, rugged **Ithaca** is the goal that gives the Odyssey its emotional center.

{#0F766E}Reaching the island is not enough: Odysseus must also recover his identity, family, and household.{/}

## City of the great war 🏛️

Troy withstands a **ten-year Greek siege** before falling through the wooden-horse deception.

{#0F766E}The Iliad shows Trojans as human and sympathetic, not faceless villains.{/}

## Daedalus's maze-like prison

King Minos commissions it to hide the **Minotaur**. Its design makes escape nearly impossible.

{#0F766E}The Labyrinth became a universal symbol of bewildering complexity.{/}

## Jason's guarded prize 🐏

The Fleece hangs in a sacred grove in **Colchis**, watched by a sleepless dragon.

{#0F766E}It represents kingship, legitimacy, and a nearly unreachable quest.{/}

## Divine protection 🛡️

The aegis is a fearsome shield or protective garment carried by **Zeus or Athena**.

{#0F766E}With Medusa's head at its center, Athena's aegis can terrify enemies.{/}

## Food and drink of the gods 🍯

**Ambrosia and nectar** sustain divine vitality and mark the difference between gods and mortals.

{#0F766E}Usually ambrosia is food and nectar drink, but ancient usage varies.{/}

## A path back through confusion 🧵

Theseus ties and unwinds the thread while entering the Labyrinth, then follows it out.

{#0F766E}“Ariadne's thread” now means a method or clue that solves a complicated problem.{/}

## Power over sea and earth 🔱

Poseidon's trident can **churn waves, shake the ground, and strike water from rock**.

{#0F766E}It is his clearest visual symbol and originally resembles a fisherman's spear.{/}

## Forged by the Cyclopes ⚡

Zeus receives the thunderbolt during the **Titanomachy** and uses it to defeat divine enemies.

{#0F766E}It becomes the supreme symbol of his sky power and kingship.{/}

## A weapon disguised as a gift 🐴

The horse carries hidden Greek soldiers **inside Troy's walls**.

{#0F766E}Today a “Trojan horse” means something apparently harmless that secretly introduces danger.{/}

## Pride that oversteps limits

**Hubris** is not ordinary confidence but arrogant behavior that ignores human limits or divine order.

{#0F766E}Arachne and Niobe are classic examples; nemesis is the answering consequence.{/}

## Sacred guest-friendship 🏠

**Xenia** requires generosity from hosts and good conduct from guests, even before identities are known.

{#0F766E}The Odyssey contrasts good hospitality with the Cyclops and the suitors' serious abuses.{/}

## The “navel” of the world

The omphalos is Delphi's sacred stone and a symbol of its place at the **center of the Greek world**.

{#0F766E}In myth, two eagles sent by Zeus meet above the spot.{/}
